DATA={'V/19': {'intro': 'Integral dependence generalizes algebraicity from field extensions to ring extensions. An element is integral when it satisfies a monic polynomial, and integrality is stable under finite algebraic constructions.', 'sections': [('Integral elements', 'An element $b$ of an $A$-algebra $B$ is integral over $A$ if it satisfies a monic polynomial with coefficients in $A$.'), ('Integral extensions', 'An extension $A\\subset B$ is integral when every element of $B$ is integral over $A$.'), ('Finite module criterion', 'An element is integral iff the subalgebra it generates is finite as an $A$-module.'), ('Finite algebras', 'Every finite $A$-algebra is integral over $A$.'), ('Transitivity', 'Integrality is transitive through towers of ring extensions.'), ('Integral elements form a ring', 'The set of elements of $B$ integral over $A$ is a subring.'), ('Determinant trick', 'Finite-generation plus stability under multiplication produces monic equations by a determinant argument.'), ('Prime behavior preview', 'Integral extensions satisfy lying-over and contraction properties for prime ideals.'), ('Examples', 'Algebraic integers, roots of monic equations, and normalization rings illustrate integral dependence.')], 'theorems': [('Finite module criterion', 'For $b\\in B$, $b$ is integral over $A$ iff $A[b]$ is finitely generated as an $A$-module.', 'A monic equation expresses every high power of $b$ in terms of lower powers. Conversely, if finitely many powers generate an $A$-module stable under multiplication by $b$, the determinant trick gives a monic polynomial annihilating $b$.'), ('Finite algebra implies integral', 'If $B$ is a finite $A$-module and an $A$-algebra, then every $b\\in B$ is integral over $A$.', 'Multiplication by $b$ is an $A$-linear endomorphism of the finite module $B$. Apply the determinant trick or Cayley--Hamilton to obtain a monic polynomial with coefficients in $A$ annihilating $b$.'), ('Transitivity of integrality', 'If $B$ is integral over $A$ and $C$ integral over $B$, then $C$ is integral over $A$.', 'For $c\\in C$, its monic equation uses finitely many coefficients from $B$. Those coefficients generate a finite $A$-algebra because they are integral; adjoining $c$ is finite over that finite algebra, hence finite over $A$, so $c$ is integral over $A$.')]}, 'V/20': {'intro': 'Integral closure collects all elements of a fraction field integral over the base ring. Normalization repairs non-normal domains by adjoining these integral elements.', 'sections': [('Integrally closed domains', 'A domain is integrally closed when every element of its fraction field integral over the ring already lies in the ring.'), ('Integral closure', 'The integral closure of $A$ in an extension field consists of all elements integral over $A$.'), ('Normalization', 'For a domain, normalization is its integral closure in its fraction field.'), ('UFDs and normality', 'Unique factorization domains are integrally closed.'), ('Localization', 'Integral closure behaves well under localization.'), ('Finite normalization', 'For many finitely generated algebras over fields, normalization is finite, though this requires hypotheses.'), ('Singular examples', 'Cusp-like rings such as $k[t^2,t^3]$ normalize to $k[t]$.'), ('Prime spectra', 'Normalization induces an integral map on spectra and separates certain singular algebraic behaviors.'), ('Conductor', 'The conductor measures the largest ideal shared by a ring and its normalization.')], 'theorems': [('UFDs are integrally closed', 'Every unique factorization domain is integrally closed in its fraction field.', 'Write an integral element as $a/b$ in lowest terms. A monic equation implies $a^n$ is divisible by $b$; coprimality and unique factorization force $b$ to be a unit.'), ('Localization of integral closure', 'If $B$ is the integral closure of a domain $A$ in a field extension and $S\\subset A$ is multiplicative, then $S^{-1}B$ is the integral closure of $S^{-1}A$ in the same localized field.', 'Localization preserves monic equations, giving one inclusion. For the reverse, clear finitely many denominators in a monic equation to show a suitable multiple of the element is integral over $A$, hence lies in $B$.'), ('Normalization of a cusp ring', 'The normalization of $k[t^2,t^3]$ in $k(t)$ is $k[t]$.', 'The element $t$ is integral because it satisfies $X^2-t^2=0$ with coefficient $t^2$ in the subring. Since $k[t]$ is a PID and therefore integrally closed, no larger integral subring inside $k(t)$ is needed.')]}, 'V/21': {'intro': 'Valuation rings impose a total divisibility order on a field. They are integrally closed local domains and provide a powerful way to test integrality and dominate local rings.', 'sections': [('Valuations', 'A valuation assigns ordered-group values compatible with products and satisfying a triangle inequality for sums.'), ('Valuation rings', 'A subring $V$ of a field $K$ is a valuation ring when for every nonzero $x\\in K$, either $x$ or $x^{-1}$ lies in $V$.'), ('Locality', 'The nonunits of a valuation ring form its unique maximal ideal.'), ('Divisibility order', 'Principal ideals are totally ordered by inclusion in a valuation ring.'), ('Discrete valuation rings', 'A DVR corresponds to a discrete rank-one valuation and has a principal maximal ideal.'), ('Integrally closed property', 'Every valuation ring is integrally closed in its fraction field.'), ('Dominating local rings', 'Valuation rings can contain a local domain while having maximal ideal contracting to the local maximal ideal.'), ('Prime ideals', 'Prime ideals of a valuation ring are linearly ordered.'), ('Integral closure criterion', 'Integral closure of a domain can be recovered as an intersection of valuation rings containing it.')], 'theorems': [('Valuation rings are local', 'If $V$ is a valuation ring of a field, its nonunits form an ideal and hence the unique maximal ideal.', 'For nonunits $a,b$, compare $a/b$ or $b/a$ when both are nonzero; one divides the other, so their sum is a multiple of one nonunit. Multiplication by arbitrary ring elements preserves nonunits.'), ('Principal ideals are totally ordered', 'For nonzero $a,b\\in V$, either $(a)\\subseteq(b)$ or $(b)\\subseteq(a)$.', 'Apply the valuation-ring condition to $a/b$: if $a/b\\in V$, then $a\\in(b)$; otherwise $b/a\\in V$, so $b\\in(a)$.'), ('Valuation rings are integrally closed', 'A valuation ring $V$ is integrally closed in its fraction field.', 'If $x$ is integral over $V$ but $x\\notin V$, then $x^{-1}\\in V$ and is a nonunit. Divide a monic equation for $x$ by the highest power of $x$ to express $1$ as an element of the maximal ideal generated by powers of $x^{-1}$, contradiction.')]}, 'V/22': {'intro': 'Chain complexes organize sequences of modules with consecutive compositions zero. Homology measures the failure of exactness and is the central invariant of homological algebra.', 'sections': [('Chain complexes', 'A chain complex has modules $C_n$ and differentials $d_n:C_n\\to C_{n-1}$ with $d_{n-1}d_n=0$.'), ('Cycles and boundaries', 'Cycles are kernels of differentials; boundaries are images of the next differentials.'), ('Homology', '$H_n(C)=\\ker d_n/\\operatorname{im}d_{n+1}$.'), ('Exact complexes', 'A complex is exact exactly when all homology modules vanish.'), ('Chain maps', 'A chain map commutes with differentials and therefore sends cycles to cycles and boundaries to boundaries.'), ('Induced maps', 'Every chain map induces maps on homology.'), ('Short exact sequences of complexes', 'Degreewise short exact sequences produce long exact sequences in homology.'), ('Homotopies', 'Chain-homotopic maps induce the same map on homology.'), ('Examples', 'Two-term complexes, resolutions, Koszul-style complexes, and singular-like algebraic complexes illustrate the formalism.')], 'theorems': [('Chain maps induce homology maps', 'A chain map $f:C\\to D$ induces $H_n(f):H_n(C)\\to H_n(D)$.', 'Commutation with differentials sends cycles to cycles. It also sends boundaries to boundaries because $f_n d_{n+1}=d_{n+1}f_{n+1}$, so the map descends to the quotient.'), ('Exactness equals zero homology', 'A chain complex is exact at every degree iff $H_n(C)=0$ for all $n$.', 'By definition, zero homology at degree $n$ means the cycle module equals the boundary module, which is exactly exactness at that term.'), ('Chain homotopic maps agree on homology', 'If $f-g=dh+hd$, then $f$ and $g$ induce the same homology maps.', 'For a cycle $z$, $(f-g)(z)=d h(z)$ because $d(z)=0$, so the difference of images is a boundary.')]}, 'V/23': {'intro': 'Free resolutions replace a module by an exact complex of free modules. They allow non-free modules to be studied using explicit matrices and are the computational input for Tor and Ext.', 'sections': [('Augmented complexes', 'A resolution ends in a surjection $F_0\\to M$ and is exact before and at $M$.'), ('Free resolutions', 'All modules $F_i$ in a free resolution are free.'), ('Construction by kernels', 'Choose free generators for $M$, then free generators for the kernel, and repeat.'), ('Projective resolutions', 'Projective resolutions generalize free resolutions and are often sufficient for derived functors.'), ('Comparison maps', 'A module homomorphism lifts to a chain map between projective resolutions.'), ('Homotopy uniqueness', 'Two such lifts are chain homotopic.'), ('Length', 'Finite projective dimension means a resolution terminates after finitely many nonzero projectives.'), ('Tensoring resolutions', 'Tensoring a free resolution computes Tor homology.'), ('Applying Hom', 'Applying Hom to a projective resolution computes Ext cohomology.'), ('Examples', 'Cyclic quotients and regular sequences yield explicit short or structured resolutions.')], 'theorems': [('Existence of free resolutions', 'Every module admits a free resolution.', 'Choose a free module surjecting onto the module. Take its kernel, choose a free module surjecting onto that kernel, and iterate indefinitely.'), ('Comparison theorem', 'A homomorphism $M\\to N$ lifts to a chain map from any projective resolution of $M$ to any resolution of $N$ ending in a surjection.', 'Lift degree zero using projectivity. Inductively, the image of the previous differential lands in the next kernel by the chain condition, and projectivity lifts through the corresponding surjection.'), ('Homotopy uniqueness of comparison maps', 'Two chain maps between projective resolutions inducing the same module map are chain homotopic.', 'Construct the homotopy inductively. At each degree, the difference of the two maps has image in a kernel already identified with the image of the next differential, and projectivity provides a lift.')]}, 'V/24': {'intro': 'Syzygies are relations among generators, then relations among relations, and so on. Iterating syzygies turns presentations into resolutions and exposes hidden structure in modules.', 'sections': [('First syzygy', 'Given a surjection $F_0\\to M$, the first syzygy module is its kernel.'), ('Higher syzygies', 'The $n$th syzygy is the kernel at stage $n-1$ of a chosen free or projective resolution.'), ('Dependence on presentation', 'Syzygies depend on choices, but stable projective information is controlled up to adding free summands.'), ('Presentations', 'A finite presentation gives a finitely generated first syzygy.'), ('Noetherian setting', 'Over a Noetherian ring, syzygies of finite modules in finite free presentations remain finitely generated.'), ('Dimension shifting', 'Higher Tor and Ext groups can be expressed through lower groups of syzygies.'), ('Regular sequences', 'Koszul-type relations provide canonical syzygies for complete-intersection situations.'), ('Hilbert syzygy preview', 'Over polynomial rings over fields, finite modules have bounded projective resolution length.'), ('Computational matrices', 'Syzygies are kernels of matrices and can be studied algorithmically.')], 'theorems': [('Syzygies over Noetherian rings are finite', 'If $A$ is Noetherian and $M$ is finitely generated, then the kernel of a map $A^n\\to M$ is finitely generated.', 'The finite free module $A^n$ is Noetherian, so every submodule, including the kernel, is finitely generated.'), ('Schanuel lemma', "If $0\\to K\\to P\\to M\\to0$ and $0\\to K'\\to P'\\to M\\to0$ have projective $P,P'$, then $K\\oplus P'\\cong K'\\oplus P$.", "Form the pullback of $P\\to M$ and $P'\\to M$. Its two projections fit into short exact sequences that split because $P$ and $P'$ are projective, yielding the two decompositions."), ('Dimension shifting for Tor', 'If $0\\to\\Omega M\\to P\\to M\\to0$ with $P$ projective, then $\\operatorname{Tor}_{n}^A(M,N)\\cong\\operatorname{Tor}_{n-1}^A(\\Omega M,N)$ for $n\\ge2$.', 'Apply the long exact Tor sequence. Higher Tor of the projective module $P$ vanishes, so adjacent terms give the stated isomorphism.')]}, 'V/25': {'intro': 'Minimal resolutions remove redundant free summands. Over local rings, minimality is detected by differentials whose matrix entries lie in the maximal ideal, making Betti numbers intrinsic.', 'sections': [('Minimal free resolutions', 'A free resolution is minimal when no contractible free summand can be removed.'), ('Local criterion', 'Over a local ring $(A,\\mathfrak m)$, a finite free resolution is minimal iff each differential has image contained in $\\mathfrak m$ times the previous free module.'), ('Reduction modulo the maximal ideal', 'Minimal differentials become zero after tensoring with the residue field.'), ('Betti numbers', 'Ranks of the free modules in a minimal resolution are invariants of the module.'), ('Nakayama mechanism', 'Minimal generating sets are detected after reduction modulo $\\mathfrak m$.'), ('Uniqueness', 'Minimal free resolutions over local rings are unique up to chain isomorphism.'), ('Projective dimension', 'The last nonzero module in a minimal finite resolution detects projective dimension.'), ('Graded analogue', 'Over standard graded rings, minimality is similarly controlled by positive-degree entries.'), ('Computational significance', 'Minimal resolutions expose generators, first relations, and higher relations without algebraic cancellation.')], 'theorems': [('Local minimality criterion', 'A free resolution $F_\\bullet$ over a local ring is minimal iff $d(F_n)\\subseteq\\mathfrak mF_{n-1}$ for all $n$.', 'If a matrix entry is a unit, elementary basis changes split off an identity map between rank-one free summands, producing a contractible piece. Conversely, if all entries lie in the maximal ideal, no such unit cancellation is possible.'), ('Betti numbers from Tor', 'For a minimal free resolution of a finite module over a local ring with residue field $k$, $\\operatorname{Tor}_n^A(M,k)\\cong F_n\\otimes_A k$.', 'Tensor the minimal resolution with $k$. Every differential becomes zero because its entries lie in $\\mathfrak m$, so homology in degree $n$ is the entire vector space $F_n\\otimes k$.'), ('Uniqueness of minimal resolutions', 'Minimal free resolutions of a finite module over a local ring are isomorphic as complexes.', "Comparison maps in both directions lift the identity of the module. Modulo the maximal ideal they are inverse isomorphisms on Tor, hence degreewise isomorphisms by Nakayama's lemma.")]}, 'V/26': {'intro': 'The Tor functor is the homology left behind when tensor product fails to be exact. It is computed from projective resolutions and detects torsion, non-flatness, and derived intersections.', 'sections': [('Definition', 'Choose a projective resolution $P_\\bullet\\to M$ and set $\\operatorname{Tor}_n^A(M,N)=H_n(P_\\bullet\\otimes_A N)$.'), ('Independence of resolution', 'Comparison and homotopy theorems make Tor well-defined up to canonical isomorphism.'), ('Degree zero', '$\\operatorname{Tor}_0^A(M,N)\\cong M\\otimes_A N$.'), ('Long exact sequence', 'Short exact sequences in either variable yield long exact Tor sequences.'), ('Projective vanishing', 'Higher Tor with a projective module vanishes.'), ('Flatness criterion', 'A module is flat iff $\\operatorname{Tor}_1^A(-,F)=0$ for all modules.'), ('Symmetry', 'Over a commutative ring, Tor is symmetric in its two module arguments.'), ('Cyclic calculation', 'For integers or quotient modules, short resolutions compute Tor explicitly.'), ('Dimension shifting', 'Syzygies reduce higher Tor computations to lower degrees.')], 'theorems': [('Well-definedness of Tor', 'The modules $H_n(P_\\bullet\\otimes N)$ are independent of the chosen projective resolution of $M$ up to canonical isomorphism.', 'Comparison maps between projective resolutions are unique up to chain homotopy. Tensor preserves chain homotopies, and chain-homotopic maps induce the same homology maps, yielding canonical isomorphisms.'), ('Projective vanishing', 'If $P$ is projective, then $\\operatorname{Tor}_n^A(P,N)=0$ for $n>0$.', 'Use the resolution concentrated in degree zero at $P$. The tensor complex has no positive-degree terms, so its positive homology vanishes.'), ('Flatness via Tor', 'An $A$-module $F$ is flat iff $\\operatorname{Tor}_1^A(A/I,F)=0$ for every finitely generated ideal $I$ under the standard finite-ideal criterion, and in particular iff $\\operatorname{Tor}_1^A(M,F)=0$ for all $M$.', 'Apply Tor to $0\\to I\\to A\\to A/I\\to0$. Vanishing of the connecting kernel is exactly injectivity of $I\\otimes F\\to F$; the general criterion follows from the flatness tests and long exact sequences.')]}, 'V/27': {'intro': 'The Ext functor is the derived correction to the left exact Hom functor. It classifies extensions in degree one and measures higher obstructions to lifting maps.', 'sections': [('Definition from projectives', 'Choose a projective resolution $P_\\bullet\\to M$ and set $\\operatorname{Ext}_A^n(M,N)=H^n(\\operatorname{Hom}_A(P_\\bullet,N))$.'), ('Degree zero', '$\\operatorname{Ext}_A^0(M,N)\\cong\\operatorname{Hom}_A(M,N)$.'), ('Long exact sequence', 'Short exact sequences yield long exact Ext sequences.'), ('Projective vanishing', 'Higher Ext out of a projective module is zero.'), ('Injective viewpoint', 'Ext can also be computed using injective resolutions in the second variable.'), ('Extension classes', '$\\operatorname{Ext}^1(M,N)$ classifies short exact sequences $0\\to N\\to E\\to M\\to0$ up to the appropriate equivalence.'), ('Dimension shifting', 'Syzygies reduce higher Ext to lower Ext.'), ('Local and graded variants', 'Localization and graded structures refine Ext in common Noetherian settings.'), ('Duality preview', 'Ext groups appear in duality, canonical modules, and deformation theory.')], 'theorems': [('Well-definedness of Ext', '$H^n(\\operatorname{Hom}(P_\\bullet,N))$ is independent of the chosen projective resolution of $M$.', 'Comparison maps between resolutions are unique up to chain homotopy. Applying Hom turns chain homotopies into cochain homotopies, which induce identical cohomology maps.'), ('Projective vanishing', 'If $P$ is projective, then $\\operatorname{Ext}_A^n(P,N)=0$ for $n>0$.', 'Use the projective resolution concentrated in degree zero. The resulting Hom cochain complex has no positive cohomology.'), ('Ext one classifies extensions', 'Equivalence classes of short exact sequences $0\\to N\\to E\\to M\\to0$ form a group naturally isomorphic to $\\operatorname{Ext}_A^1(M,N)$.', 'Pull an extension back along a projective presentation of $M$ and choose a splitting over the projective term. The resulting map from the first syzygy to $N$ defines a degree-one cohomology class; changing the splitting changes it by a coboundary, and the construction is reversible via pushout.')]}, 'V/28': {'intro': 'Derived functors organize Tor, Ext, and similar constructions as systematic corrections to nonexact functors. Resolutions make these corrections computable, while long exact sequences expose their functorial structure.', 'sections': [('Exact and derived behavior', 'Left or right exact functors lose information at kernels or cokernels; derived functors measure that failure in higher degrees.'), ('Left derived functors', 'Projective or flat resolutions produce left derived functors of right exact functors.'), ('Right derived functors', 'Injective resolutions produce right derived functors of left exact functors.'), ('Tor as derived tensor', '$\\operatorname{Tor}_n$ is the left derived functor of tensor product.'), ('Ext as derived Hom', '$\\operatorname{Ext}^n$ is the right derived functor of Hom in the second variable or equivalently computed projectively in the first.'), ('Universality', 'Derived functors are characterized by effaceability and universal delta-functor properties.'), ('Long exact sequences', 'Connecting morphisms package the failure of exactness into functorial long exact sequences.'), ('Acyclic resolutions', 'One may compute derived functors using suitable classes of acyclic objects, not only projectives or injectives.'), ('Derived-category preview', 'Complexes and quasi-isomorphisms provide a setting in which derived functors become ordinary functors after localization.'), ('Algebra-geometry bridge', 'Derived tensor and derived Hom underpin intersection theory, deformation theory, and sheaf cohomology.')], 'theorems': [('Tor is the left derived tensor functor', 'For fixed $N$, the homology of a projective resolution tensor $N$ gives the left derived functors of $-\\otimes_A N$.', 'Projective resolutions are acyclic for tensor in positive resolution degrees and comparison maps make the construction functorial and independent of resolution; degree zero recovers ordinary tensor.'), ('Ext is a derived Hom functor', 'For fixed $N$, cohomology of $\\operatorname{Hom}(P_\\bullet,N)$ gives derived functors extending $\\operatorname{Hom}(-,N)$ contravariantly in the first variable.', 'Projective resolutions replace the first variable by Hom-acyclic objects. Comparison and homotopy yield functorial well-defined cohomology, and degree zero recovers Hom.'), ('Derived functors produce long exact sequences', 'A short exact sequence in the appropriate variable induces a natural long exact sequence of derived functors.', 'Resolve compatibly or apply the functor to a short exact sequence of suitable resolutions. The resulting short exact sequence of complexes yields the standard long exact sequence in homology or cohomology via connecting morphisms.')]}}
